rts with a digit raises an error instead of being sent, because the\n   parser would read the digit as a duration, and a description it can't parse\n   makes Intervals wipe both representations.\n\n## Expected on the watch (LTHR 172 in Intervals)\n\n| step | % LTHR | bpm |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | 117–129 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | 151–160 |\n| Float | 75–82 | 129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | 112–124 |\n\n## Not done here (needs your account and watch)\n\nFollowing the ground rules, I didn't push anything. To verify:\n\n1. `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json && ./push.sh event.json`\n2. `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{new id}`: `workout_doc.steps` should be\n   non-empty with `start`/`end` in the HR targets. If you get `steps: []`, the\n   description parser choked.\n3. Sync through Garmin Connect and open the workout. The watch should show the\n   bpm values in the table above. Because the watch's LTHR (168) differs from\n   Intervals' (172), this proves the targets come from Intervals.\n4. Delete the old event 184233 (and the test event, if you want).\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S49jtCdRqwRBaz44HQqeav","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /private/tmp/h-mlMo5k/ANSWER.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14c9338c-5aca-48d7-9740-74a1a3d864e2","uuid":"5fe58528-2d2f-40a1-b923-a3958f96c638","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:30:56.640Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/private/tmp/h-mlMo5k/ANSWER.md","content":"# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## Cause\n\n`gen.py` wrote each HR target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\nIntervals accepts that shape, and the readback looks fine. But when it builds\nthe FIT file, `{value, end}` becomes a **Lap-HR / zone-mapped** target, so the\nwatch snaps it to its own HR zone bands (built from its auto-detected LTHR of 168).\nThat matches every row in `readback/watch-notes.md`: the watch displayed one of its\nown zones (118–137, 151–162, 137–151) and never the planned range. Changing the\nwatch's LTHR moved the bands, but they were still zone edges.\n\nThe earlier tries failed for different reasons. Absolute `bpm` gets misread at\nexport (the watch showed 214–249). `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` aren't part of the schema\nand get dropped silently (constant 101 is Garmin's \"no valid target\" fallback).\n\n## Change (`gen.py`)\n\n1. **`hr_target`: the lower bound is now `start` instead of `value`.**\n   `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}` exports as an **Instant-HR\n   corridor**. The watch then shows the raw bpm that Intervals computed from its\n   own LTHR (172) and ignores the watch's zones. Targets stay as percentages,\n   so Intervals does the conversion at export from the threshold it has stored.\n   This rename is the actual fix.\n2. **The event now gets a top-level `description`** in Intervals' text\n   grammar, so the steps show in the Intervals web UI (the UI reads\n   `description`, not `workout_doc`). For the tempo plan:\n\n   ```\n   - Warm-up easy 10m 68-75% LTHR\n\n   3x\n   - Tempo 8m 88-93% LTHR\n   - Float 2m 75-82% LTHR\n\n   - Cool-down 5m 65-72% LTHR\n   ```\n\n   It follows the grammar rules: `m` means minutes and is used only for\n   durations (non-whole minutes become `s`). There are blank lines around `Nx`\n   repeat blocks and targets are explicit `% LTHR`, never a bare `Z<n>`. A label\n   that starts with a digit raises an error instead of being sent, because the\n   parser would read the digit as a duration, and a description it can't parse\n   makes Intervals wipe both representations.\n\n## Expected on the watch (LTHR 172 in Intervals)\n\n| step | % LTHR | bpm |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | 117–129 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | 151–160 |\n| Float | 75–82 | 129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | 112–124 |\n\n## Not done here (needs your account and watch)\n\nFollowing the ground rules, I didn't push anything. To verify:\n\n1. `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json && ./push.sh event.json`\n2. `GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{new id}`: `workout_doc.steps` should be\n   non-empty with `start`/`end` in the HR targets. If you get `steps: []`, the\n   description parser choked.\n3. Sync through Garmin Connect and open the workout. The watch should show the\n   bpm values in the table above. Because the watch's LTHR (168) differs from\n   Intervals' (172), this proves the targets come from Intervals.\n4. Delete the old event 184233 (and the test event, if you want).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S49jtCdRqwRBaz44HQqeav","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAoxEwfXKKexWagT9vXm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator, but it hasn't been tested on Intervals or the watch yet. I didn't push anything, per the ground rules, so you'll need to push and sync to confirm it works.\n\n**The cause:** `gen.py` sent each HR target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. Intervals accepts that, but its FIT export turns a `{value, end}` target into a zone target. The watch then snaps it to one of its own HR zones, which is what every row in `watch-notes.md` shows.\n\n**The fix:** in `gen.py`, `hr_target` now writes the lower bound as `start` instead of `value`. With `{start, end}`, the watch should show the bpm range Intervals worked out from its own LTHR (172). The targets are still percentages, so Intervals does the conversion.\n\n**Also added, following the guide:** each event now gets a top-level `description` in Intervals' text format, so the steps show up in the Intervals web UI:\n```\n- Warm-up easy 10m 68-75% LTHR\n\n3x\n- Tempo 8m 88-93% LTHR\n- Float 2m 75-82% LTHR\n\n- Cool-down 5m 65-72% LTHR\n```\n`gen.py` now refuses to generate an event if a step label starts with a digit. Intervals would read that digit as a duration, and if it can't parse the description it wipes the whole workout.\n\n**To check it:**\n1. Push a new event.\n2. Read it back from the API: the HR targets should have `start`/`end`, and `workout_doc.steps` should not be empty. If it's empty, Intervals couldn't parse the description.\n3. Sync the watch. It should show:\n\n| Step | bpm |\n|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 117–129 |\n| Tempo | 151–160 |\n| Float | 129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 112–124 |\n\nBecause the watch's LTHR (168) differs from Intervals' (172), seeing these exact numbers proves the targets come from Intervals.\n\nAfter that, delete the old event 184233. The full explanation and these steps are in `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1375,"cache_read_input_tokens":25759,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1375},"output_tokens":703,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14c9338c-5aca-48d7-9740-74a1a3d864e2","uuid":"2c5d4809-aa45-41c7-ac37-774f17523192","timestamp":"2026-10-19T02:31:04.497Z","request_id":"req_011CgAoxEgYUZRdNpb7zSn9t"}
